  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nLooking at the h5 analysis early this morning, a ridge of mid-summer \nstrength (600 dm!) is centered over the TX/OK/AR/LA region. Around \nthe periphery of this ridge is a shortwave of southwest monsoon \norigin moving out of northern Neb and across SoDak. Widespread \nrainfall has developed with this wave, though it's your classic \nsynoptic setup, with strong WAA at h85 and fgen around h7 combining \nto create this precip shield. This will all move east across \nsouthern MN and into western WI, resulting in several hours of light \nto moderate rain across the area today. This will result in a fairly \nwidespread 1-2 inches of rain along and south of a roughly Morris \nto St. Cloud and Chippewa Falls line. MUCAPE will be limited with \nthis round of precipitation, so the excessive rain risk with this \nfirst wave is low. It will just be a much needed soaking rain and \nwashout of a Friday.\n\nThis evening is when the excessive rain threat begins to show up as \nthe warm front begins to sharpen to the north of I-80 in Iowa, with \na persistent LLJ riding up over the front. PWATs will be near 2\" \nwith MUCAPE increasing to over 2000 j/kg over northern Iowa. All the \ningredients are in place for a heavy rain event tonight to the north \nof the warm front, but the the HREF/REFS show the LLJ setting up \nfrom about Omaha to the I-90/94 split near Tomah in WI. It's along \nthe MN/IA border from I-35 east that the atmosphere looks to have in \nits crosshairs for producing heavy rain, which means it's the \nportions of far southeast MN and northeast IA in the La Crosse WFO \ncoverage area that are really under the greatest risk for seeing \nexcessive rainfall. HREF and REFS PMM QPF both show near 8\" of rain \nfalling near the MN/IA border east of I-35, which looks reasonable \ngiven the synoptic setup with this event. We already have Freeborn \nCounty in a Flood Watch for the excessive rainfall potential and \nthis looks to be our one county that has the greatest risk of seeing \nheavy rainfall, so we maintained the watch for only that county. \nBesides heavy rain, with that 2000+ j/kg of MUCAPE, HREF/REFS both \nshow some stronger updrafts with some rotation developing with this \nsecond round, so the inclusion of a Marginal Risk up toward \nRochester from the SPC in their Day 1 convective outlook makes \nsense. With that said, lapse rates look to be near moist adiabatic \nin the mid-levels, so this likely puts the hail ceiling for this \nevent at a water coated 1\". The timeframe that will have the \ngreatest risk for both excessive rainfall and severe weather in the \nMPX area will be 00z to 06z this evening (7pm to 1am).\n\nFor Sunday through Wednesday, the main feature that shows up on the \nforecast charts is persistent high pressure to the north of the \nGreat Lakes. This will keep low level flow out of the east for \nSunday through Wednesday. These east winds will be very dry below \nh7, which will help push any rainfall south and west of the MPX area \nthrough the middle of next week. We'll continue to see weak \ndisturbances ride around the southern ridge Sunday and Monday, but \nthose look to result in continued mid/upper level cloud cover, but \nwill struggle to produce more than the occasional sprinkles given \nthe dry low level air. These east winds will also keep temperatures \nseasonable, with lows in the 30s/40s and highs in the 60s. Depending \non what cloud cover looks like, it is possible we'll need some frost \nheadlines Monday and Tuesday mornings in western WI.\n\nBeyond Wednesday, the northern stream will have retreated well to \nthe north in Canada, which will keep us dry. The only really change \nwe'll see to the weather for the end of next week is our easterly \nwinds will become southeast, which will allow temperatures to warm \nup some, with the frost threat going away and highs starting to \ncreep back up into the 70s.",
